Exercise 1.15 asks for non-isomorphic infinite domains in which every element is finite and no strict chain runs through all of \(\mathbb{N}\). `flatMem` takes the neighbourhoods to be \(\mathbb{N}\) together with the singletons, and `flat` is the nested-or-disjoint system whose comparison `flatNOD` cases on whether each set is the whole set or a singleton; `stemMem` adds the pair \(\{0, 1\}\), and `stemNOD` places each singleton inside that pair or disjoint from it according as the index is \(0\) or \(1\). On the flat side the exercise hypotheses are proved. `flat_classify` says, classically, that every element is bottom or the principal filter of a singleton: one singleton in the element excludes every other, because `singleton_disjoint` makes distinct singletons meet emptily and `flat_empty_not_mem` says the empty set is not a neighbourhood, while an element with no singleton matches bottom by extensionality. `flat_all_finite` rewrites that classification as principality, so every flat element is finite. `flat_atom_maximal` says a singleton filter is maximal: an element above it is bottom or an atom, bottom cannot contain the singleton, and an atom \(\{m\}\) forces \(m = n\) by inclusion of singletons. `flat_no_three_chain` then forbids \(a < b < c\), because \(b\) is bottom, which equals \(a\), or an atom, which equals \(c\), and `flat_no_infinite_chain` observes that the first three terms of a strict \(\omega\)-sequence would form such a chain. `flat_infinite` says \(n \mapsto \uparrow\{n\}\) is injective, so the flat domain is infinite. The stem enters only as a longer chain: `stem_principal_lt` says a proper inclusion of stem neighbourhoods reverses to a strict inequality of principal filters, and `stem_three_chain` gives \(\uparrow\mathbb{N} < \uparrow\{0, 1\} < \uparrow\{0\}\), because \(2\) lies in \(\mathbb{N}\) outside the pair and \(1\) lies in the pair outside \(\{0\}\). `not_isomorphic`, exported as `example_115`, assumes an order-isomorphism, pulls that three-element chain back along the inverse, and contradicts `flat_no_three_chain`. No stem lemma shows that every stem element is finite, that the stem has infinitely many elements, or that the stem has no strict \(\omega\)-chain, which are the three conditions the exercise imposes on both domains. **Theorem:** partial — the two systems are proved non-isomorphic because the stem carries a strict three-element chain and the flat domain has none, and the stem is unchecked for finiteness of elements, infinitude, and absence of an infinite strict chain. **Proof:** partial — the flat classification, the reduction of an infinite strict sequence to its first three terms, and the transport of `stem_three_chain` along an inverse isomorphism prove the non-isomorphism, and the corresponding stem properties remain unproved.
